rrect answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338993 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }x<y<{x}^{2},\\,1<x\\le 2}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }2<y<x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }4\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1615097-eb1e-5984-b134-50e4277f6126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338993 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(2,2).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338997 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338997 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338997 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338997 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0<{y}^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<{x}^{2}<9,\\,1<{y}^{2}<4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x<1,\\,{y}^{2}<4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338997 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef298213-bf03-538b-9273-2913d7465266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338997 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,\\sqrt{2}).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378271 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378271 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378271 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378271 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }3x+y<8,\\,0<x<3}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0\\le x<6,\\,2<y\\le 4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}<6}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378271 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c082af6c-4b35-55f0-b925-7876243430ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378271 #edb/ref 1074 